### What does the naive code look like?

The naive dedup has three separate operations: a read, a side effect, and a write, with no atomicity binding them.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

public class NaiveDedup {

    private final ProcessedEventStore store;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    // BROKEN: three separate non-atomic operations
    public void handle(CreditEvent event) {
        // Step 1: READ - check if already processed
        if (!store.alreadyProcessed(event.getEventId())) {
            // Step 2: SIDE EFFECT - apply the business change
            accountRepo.credit(
                event.getAccountId(), event.getAmount());
            // Step 3: WRITE - mark as processed
            store.markProcessed(event.getEventId());
        }
    }
}
```

```text
Three separate operations:
  Step 1: alreadyProcessed(id)  -> READ
  Step 2: process()             -> SIDE EFFECT
  Step 3: markProcessed(id)     -> WRITE

  Both bugs live in the GAPS between these steps.
```

### How does the concurrency race work?

Two consumers execute `alreadyProcessed(id)` at nearly the same time. Both reads return false. Both proceed to process.

```text
Concurrency race (two consumers, same event EVT-001):

  Consumer A                    Consumer B
  ─────────                     ─────────
  alreadyProcessed(EVT-001)?    alreadyProcessed(EVT-001)?
  -> false                      -> false
                                (neither has marked yet!)
  credit Priya 50000            credit Priya 50000
  markProcessed(EVT-001)        markProcessed(EVT-001)

  Result: Priya credited 100000 instead of 50000!
  The read and the mark are not atomic.
```

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

// Simulating the race
public class RaceDemo {

    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // Both threads check at the same time
        // Thread A: alreadyProcessed? -> false
        // Thread B: alreadyProcessed? -> false (not yet marked!)
        // Thread A: credit Priya
        // Thread B: credit Priya (duplicate!)
        // Thread A: markProcessed
        // Thread B: markProcessed
        System.out.println("Race condition: Priya credited TWICE");
    }
}
```

### How does the crash failure work?

The consumer processes the business change but crashes before marking. On redelivery, the ID is still unmarked.

```text
Crash failure (single consumer, event EVT-001):

  Step 1: alreadyProcessed(EVT-001)? -> false
  Step 2: credit Priya 50000         -> COMMITTED
  *** CRASH ***
  Step 3: markProcessed(EVT-001)     -> NEVER EXECUTED

  Redelivery (broker resends EVT-001):
  Step 1: alreadyProcessed(EVT-001)? -> false (never marked!)
  Step 2: credit Priya 50000         -> COMMITTED AGAIN
  Result: Priya credited 100000 instead of 50000!
```

### Can reordering the lines fix it?

No. Reordering moves the bug but does not eliminate it.

```text
Attempt: mark first, then process
  Step 1: markProcessed(EVT-001)
  *** CRASH ***
  Step 2: process() -> NEVER EXECUTED
  Result: event marked but never processed -> money lost!

Attempt: check, mark, then process
  Step 1: alreadyProcessed? -> false
  Step 2: markProcessed(EVT-001)
  *** CRASH ***
  Step 3: process() -> NEVER EXECUTED
  Result: same problem, just a different gap

  The fix is NOT reordering. The fix is ATOMICITY.
```

### Fix 1: How does an atomic INSERT with UNIQUE constraint work?

Instead of reading then writing, attempt a single atomic INSERT. The database guarantees only one insert succeeds.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import org.springframework.dao.DuplicateKeyException;
import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class AtomicClaimConsumer {

    private final InboxRepository inbox;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public AtomicClaimConsumer(InboxRepository inbox,
                                AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.inbox = inbox;
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional
    public void handle(CreditEvent event) {
        try {
            // Atomic claim: INSERT with UNIQUE(event_id)
            inbox.insert(event.getEventId());
        } catch (DuplicateKeyException e) {
            // Another consumer already claimed this event
            return; // Skip safely
        }

        // Only the winner reaches here
        accountRepo.credit(
            event.getAccountId(), event.getAmount());
    }
}
```

```text
Atomic claim under concurrency:
  Consumer A: INSERT EVT-001 -> success (wins)
  Consumer B: INSERT EVT-001 -> DuplicateKeyException (loses)
  Consumer B: skips processing
  Result: Priya credited exactly once
```

### Fix 2: How does a single transaction prevent crash double-processing?

Commit the business change and the inbox record together. Only two outcomes: both present or neither present.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class TransactionalInboxConsumer {

    private final InboxRepository inbox;
    private final AccountRepository accountRepo;

    public TransactionalInboxConsumer(InboxRepository inbox,
                                       AccountRepository accountRepo) {
        this.inbox = inbox;
        this.accountRepo = accountRepo;
    }

    @Transactional  // Both in same transaction
    public void handle(CreditEvent event) {
        if (inbox.existsByEventId(event.getEventId())) {
            return;
        }

        // Business change
        accountRepo.credit(
            event.getAccountId(), event.getAmount());

        // Dedup record (same transaction)
        inbox.save(new InboxEntry(event.getEventId()));

        // COMMIT: both together, or neither
        // Crash before commit: neither happened -> redelivery safe
        // Crash after commit: both present -> duplicate detected
    }
}
```

```text
Same-transaction guarantee:
  Crash BEFORE commit: credit not applied, inbox not written
    -> redelivery processes from scratch (safe)
  Crash AFTER commit: credit applied, inbox written
    -> redelivery finds inbox entry, skips (safe)
  No in-between state where credit is applied but inbox is missing!
```

### How do the two fixes work together?

| Bug | Root Cause | Fix |
|---|---|---|
| Concurrency race | Check and mark are separate | UNIQUE constraint on event_id |
| Crash double-process | Process and mark are separate | Same database transaction |
| Both combined | Three non-atomic operations | Atomic INSERT + same TX |

### What about external side effects?

When the side effect is external (email, API call), you cannot include it in the database transaction. Record the intent in the transaction and perform the external action idempotently afterward.

```java
package com.bank.consumer;

import org.springframework.stereotype.Service;
import org.springframework.transaction.annotation.Transactional;

@Service
public class ConsumerWithExternalEffect {

    @Transactional
    public void handle(CreditEvent event) {
        if (inbox.exists(event.getEventId())) return;

        // Database effects in transaction
        accountRepo.credit(event.getAccountId(), event.getAmount());
        inbox.save(event.getEventId());

        // Record intent for external effect
        outbox.save(new EmailIntent(
            event.getAccountId(), "Credit received"));
    }

    // Separate process reads outbox, sends email idempotently,
    // marks done. If email fails, outbox entry remains for retry.
}
```

```text
External effects:
  Cannot join DB transaction -> record intent in outbox
  Outbox processor sends email and marks done
  If email service is idempotent -> safe to retry
  If not -> accept at-most-once for that effect
```
